# Which browsers the bundle is compiled for (issue #321).
#
# Without this file the target is Next.js's default — Chrome 64, Edge 79,
# Firefox 67, Safari 12 — and SWC down-levels our own source for browsers that
# cannot run this product at all. Docify converts nothing without WebAssembly
# and a Web Worker, and its engines need more than that again:
# `createImageBitmap` and `OffscreenCanvas` for the canvas path,
# `SharedArrayBuffer` behind cross-origin isolation for the threaded builds,
# `VideoEncoder`/`VideoDecoder` for the WebCodecs path. Safari shipped neither
# `OffscreenCanvas` nor WebCodecs until 16.4, so a Safari 12 that is handed
# down-levelled bytes reaches the converter and is turned away by `route()`
# anyway.
#
# ## What this is measured to be worth
#
# Two clean builds on the same machine, 2026-09-17, totalled over every file in
# `.next/static/chunks`:
#
#   without this file   4 890 417 bytes raw   1 628 570 gzipped
#   with it             4 882 624 bytes raw   1 626 543 gzipped
#
# 7.8 kB raw, 2.0 kB gzipped. Real, and small.
#
# ## What it is *not* worth, against what #321 expected
#
# It does not clear Lighthouse's `legacy-javascript` audit, and no browserslist
# can. The polyfills that audit names — `Array.prototype.at`, `Object.hasOwn`,
# `String.prototype.trimStart` and the rest — are not compiler output. They are
# `next/dist/build/polyfills/polyfill-module.js`, which
# `next/dist/client/app-globals.js` requires unconditionally, so they are in the
# bundle whatever the target says. That module is 1380 bytes, not the 11.7 kB
# Lighthouse estimates; the audit sizes a polyfill by what core-js would have
# spent on it, and Next hand-wrote these.
#
# Removing it is possible — a webpack alias onto an empty module — and is
# deliberately not done here. It also defines `URL.canParse`, which is Safari
# 17, well above the floor below, and it is an internal path a Next upgrade may
# move without telling us. 1380 bytes is not worth either.
#
# ## Why these floors
#
# `Object.hasOwn` and `Array.prototype.at`: Chrome 93, Firefox 92, Safari 15.4.
#
# That is the level at which every method Next's polyfill module covers is
# natively present *except* `URL.canParse` — and since the module stays, that
# one goes on being polyfilled for everybody and so does not set anything. It is
# the newest method the floors have to clear, not the newest the module carries.
#
# The floors are deliberately lower than the engines require. Any browser that
# clears them but not WebCodecs or `OffscreenCanvas` still reads every static
# page, and meets `route()` rather than a broken script if it reaches the
# converter. Raising them to what the converter needs would buy no bytes and
# cost readers.
#
# What the bytes actually come from is syntax, not these methods: SWC
# down-levels our own source to the target, and does not inject method
# polyfills. The method list is what says the floor is *safe*; the syntax is
# what makes it worth setting.
#
# Read by Next through `browserslist.loadConfig` — `next/dist/build/utils.js` —
# and passed to SWC as its target. Raising a floor is a product decision about
# who can open the site, which is why it is here and not among the headers in
# `next.config.ts`.

chrome >= 93
edge >= 93
firefox >= 92
safari >= 15.4
ios_saf >= 15.4
opera >= 79
samsung >= 17
and_chr >= 93
and_ff >= 92
not dead
